// Data-loss prevention: scrub structured secrets out of traffic in place, at the
// NIC, so they never reach the backend (or, watching the server's replies, never
// leave it). `redact`/`replace` take a `regex` as well as a literal — but a
// rewrite overwrites a span in place, so the regex must be FIXED-LENGTH (the
// match end fixes the start). Variable-length patterns (e.g. a bare email) are
// rejected at compile time; use a fixed shape.
//
// Here, on the plaintext hop to and from the app on :8080: fill any dashed SSN
// or dashed card number with a filler byte, and overwrite a GitHub personal
// access token with a same-length placeholder. The L4 checksum is fixed up
// automatically.
//
// These are SHAPE matches, not validation: any ddd-dd-dddd run is filled, whether
// or not it is a real SSN, and an undashed number is not touched. Matching is
// case-sensitive on purpose (no `nocase`): the token prefix is lower-case. A
// match split across two segments is not rewritten (a rewrite works on one
// packet). All the regexes of a program compile into ONE DFA, which has to fit
// the kernel's 1024-state table: a token whose prefix letters can also occur in
// its own body (an AWS `AKIA[A-Z0-9]{16}`) costs ~2500 states alone, while the
// `_` in `ghp_` keeps this one small.
regex ssn  = "\d{3}-\d{2}-\d{4}";             // 123-45-6789  (11 bytes)
regex card = "\d{4}-\d{4}-\d{4}-\d{4}";        // 4111-1111-1111-1111 (19 bytes)
regex pat  = "ghp_[A-Za-z0-9]{36}";            // a GitHub personal access token (40 bytes)
filter {
  if (proto != 6) decide pass;
  if (dport != 8080 && sport != 8080) decide pass;   // requests to, and replies from, the app
  redact(ssn);                                 // -> ***********
  redact(card, 'X');                            // -> XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX
  replace(pat, "ghp_XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX");   // same-length placeholder
  decide pass;
}
